cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use webgpu-fly in academic work, please cite as below."
type: software
title: "webgpu-fly: A real Drosophila brain, spinal cord, and body running in a browser tab via WebGPU"
authors:
  - family-names: Günaydın
    given-names: Ahmet Barış
    email: hi@barisgunaydin.com
    affiliation: Independent researcher
repository-code: "https://github.com/abgnydn/webgpu-fly"
url: "https://webgpu-fly.pages.dev"
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-06-05
keywords:
  - WebGPU
  - connectome
  - Drosophila
  - FlyWire
  - leaky integrate-and-fire
  - spiking neural network
  - MuJoCo
  - reinforcement learning
  - embodiment
  - ventral nerve cord
  - in-browser computation
  - reproducible research
abstract: >-
  webgpu-fly runs a whole-animal Drosophila nervous system entirely in a
  web browser. The FlyWire FAFB brain connectome (139,255 neurons, ~15M
  synaptic connections) and the Janelia MANC ventral-nerve-cord
  connectome (23,188 neurons, 5.2M connections) are each simulated as
  leaky integrate-and-fire networks in fused WebGPU compute kernels, with
  presynaptic-neurotransmitter signs baked into the weights so the inner
  loop never branches on excitatory/inhibitory type. Brain command
  neurons drive the spine by cell-type name match; the spine's motor
  neurons drive a physically simulated 67-body, 111-actuator TuragaLab
  flybody model running in MuJoCo compiled to WebAssembly. A 64x16 retina
  rendered from the fly's head pose feeds back into the brain's optic
  neurons. An optional trained reinforcement-learning walking policy
  (Vaxenburg et al. 2025) runs as a pure-TypeScript forward pass verified
  element-wise against the published checkpoint. The project is a game
  with shareable, deterministic replay URLs: a shared link re-executes
  the identical neuron cascade against the same connectome. The brain LIF
  kernel is memory-bandwidth-bound and runs at ~0.25 kHz biological time
  on an Apple M2 Pro, benchmarked honestly against NEST 3.10 (0.67 kHz)
  and a hand-written multicore Rust port (0.45 kHz) on the same machine;
  the contribution is reachability (a real connectome simulation behind a
  single URL), not raw throughput.
